**Proof.**

We prove that for every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $R > 0$ such that $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$ for all $x \ge R$. Fix $\varepsilon > 0$.

Consider the interval $[1,2] \subset (0,\infty)$. For each integer $m \ge 1$ define
$$
S_m := \bigl\{\alpha \in [1,2] : |f(n\alpha)| \le \varepsilon \text{ for all } n \ge m\bigr\}.
$$

**Claim 1.** $\bigcup_{m=1}^{\infty} S_m = [1,2]$.

Indeed, by hypothesis $\lim_{n\to\infty} f(n\alpha) = 0$ for every $\alpha > 0$. Hence for each $\alpha \in [1,2]$ there exists $m$ (depending on $\alpha$) such that $|f(n\alpha)| \le \varepsilon$ for all $n \ge m$, i.e. $\alpha \in S_m$.

**Claim 2.** Each $S_m$ is closed.

The map $\alpha \mapsto f(n\alpha)$ is continuous (composition of $\alpha \mapsto n\alpha$ with $f$), so $\{\alpha \in [1,2] : |f(n\alpha)| \le \varepsilon\}$ is closed for each $n$. Thus
$$
S_m = \bigcap_{n \ge m} \{\alpha \in [1,2] : |f(n\alpha)| \le \varepsilon\}
$$
is an intersection of closed sets, hence closed (in $[1,2]$).

**Applying Baire's category theorem.** The interval $[1,2]$ is a complete metric space, and by Claims 1–2 it is a countable union of closed sets $S_m$. By Baire's theorem, some $S_{m_0}$ has nonempty interior; in particular there exist $c, d$ with
$$
1 \le c < d \le 2 \quad\text{and}\quad [c,d] \subset S_{m_0}.
$$
Consequently
$$
|f(n\alpha)| \le \varepsilon \quad\text{for all } \alpha \in [c,d] \text{ and all } n \ge m_0. \tag{$\ast$}
$$

**Covering a tail of $[0,\infty)$.** Since $d > c$, choose an integer $N \ge m_0$ with $N > \dfrac{c}{d-c}$. Then for every $n \ge N$,
$$
nd \ge (n+1)c \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad n(d-c) \ge c,
$$
which holds by the choice of $N$. Hence the intervals $[nc, nd]$ overlap consecutively for $n \ge N$, and since $nd \to \infty$,
$$
\bigcup_{n \ge N} [nc, nd] = [Nc, \infty).
$$

Every $x \ge Nc$ lies in some $[nc, nd]$ with $n \ge N \ge m_0$, so $x = n\alpha$ for some $\alpha \in [c,d]$, and by $(\ast)$,
$$
|f(x)| = |f(n\alpha)| \le \varepsilon.
$$

Thus $|f(x)| \le \varepsilon$ for all $x \ge Nc$.

Since $\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary, we conclude
$$
\lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) = 0. \qquad\blacksquare
$$

